# control-community-withdraw-egress -- the GENERAL forward rail's copy of
# rs-control-community-withdraw-egress.ci, which asserts the same behavior on the
# route-server fast-path rail.
#
# WHY BOTH. reactorForwardRS (forward_rs.go) and forwardUpdateCore
# (reactor_api_forward.go) both answer the RFC 7947 control-community question, and which
# one answers it is decided by the SOURCE peer's rs-fast-path flag, not by anything the
# operator asked about withdrawals. The two rails MUST therefore stay behaviorally
# identical, and they did not: the route-server rail sent a refused client its withdrawals
# while this rail dropped them. This file is the copy of that assertion with the fast path
# OFF, so a fix landing on one rail only cannot go green.
#
# WHAT IT GUARDS. A ze route-server control community names a whitelist or a blacklist of
# CLIENT ASNs, and wireu.ParseCommunityPolicy reads it off the ANNOUNCED route's COMMUNITY
# attribute. CommunityPolicy.ShouldForwardTo is therefore a decision about ONE ROUTE, while
# the rail applied it to a whole MESSAGE. One UPDATE can carry both halves -- RFC 7606
# Section 5.1 tells a sender not to emit that shape and ze must still handle it on receipt
# (wireu.MixesNLRIFields) -- and the Withdrawn Routes field carries no attributes, so it was
# tagged by nobody. Refusing the whole message leaves the excluded client holding a prefix
# an earlier UPDATE did advertise to it and ze can no longer take back until the session
# resets.
#
# RFC 7947 requires per-client import and export policy and places no normative requirement
# on these communities: the control convention is ze's own (wireu.StripControlCommunities).
# The obligation this file asserts is therefore ze's, not the RFC's, and it carries no
# `RFC requirement:` tag.
#
# WHY A .ci AND NOT ONLY THE UNIT TESTS. The unit tests
# (TestForwardWithdrawsFromClientRefusedByControlCommunity, and the rails-agree test beside
# it) prove the rail splits the message. Only a running daemon proves the withdrawal reaches
# a socket while the announcement does not, and the egress rail is where a stranded route
# actually happens.
#
# NON-VACUITY (ai/rules/interop-and-goal-validation.md). Three controls, and the reject rule
# covers the leak in the other direction:
#   1. the FENCE route 192.0.2.0/24 IS delivered to the excluded client, so a build
#      forwarding nothing to it is red;
#   2. the client the community does NOT name receives BOTH halves plus the fence, so a
#      build suppressing on the wrong side is red;
#   3. the observer counts the excluded client's UPDATEs and refuses a third one, so the
#      announcement leaking is red on the counter as well as on the wire.
#
# ORDERING. The source sends the mixed UPDATE BEFORE the fence, on one TCP stream ze
# processes in order on one goroutine. The observer waiting for the fence therefore proves
# the mixed UPDATE was already decided.

# ONE peer process holds all three sessions, and the conn number is the peer's address
# rather than the order ze happened to connect in (option=conn_map, peer_connmap.go). Three
# separate ze-peer processes would be simpler to read and are NOT usable here: the barrier
# below is per-batch, so it exists only inside one process.
stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=3
option=timeout:value=30s
option=bind:value=0.0.0.0
# conn=1 is 127.0.0.1 (the source), conn=2 is 127.0.0.2 (excluded), conn=3 is 127.0.0.3
# (included): remote-ip sorts the accepted batch by TCP source address, which is each peer's
# `local ip` below.
option=conn_map:value=remote-ip
# THE BARRIER, and this test is unsound without it. ze emits its End-of-RIB to a peer only
# after that peer's initial sync, so the EoR is the first frame proving the peer is a live
# forward target. Without the barrier the source's UPDATE can be forwarded while a client is
# still coming up, and that client then loses the WITHDRAWAL for good: an announcement is
# replayed from the Adj-RIB-In when the session comes up, and a withdrawal has nothing to
# replay from. Measured before the barrier was added: this file and its route-server sibling
# each failed about one run in four, always on the client that lost the withdrawal. Same
# reasoning, and the same option, as rfc7606-relay-one-field.ci.
option=await_eor:value=true
# The peers must outlive the assertions: a source that closes makes ze withdraw its routes,
# which would deliver the clients messages they never asked for.
option=linger:value=true

# conn=1, the SOURCE -- external, AS 65001, on 127.0.0.1. NO rs-fast-path in its config
# below: that flag is what selects the route-server rail, so its absence is what puts this
# test on the general one.
#
# seq=1 -- the MIXED UPDATE. Withdrawn Routes = 198.51.100.0/24 (18C63364). NLRI =
# 10.0.0.0/24 (180A0000) with ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH=[65001], NEXT_HOP=1.1.1.1,
# LOCAL_PREF=100, COMMUNITY=0:65002 (0000FDEA). The 0:X form is the ze route-server
# BLACKLIST convention (wireu.parseCommunityAttr), so ShouldForwardTo answers false for the
# client in AS 65002 and true for the one in AS 65003. The community rides in the attribute
# section, which governs the NLRI only: the Withdrawn Routes field carries no attributes.
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF004102000418C6336400224001010040020602010000FDE94003040101010140050400000064C008040000FDEA180A0000
# seq=2 -- the FENCE, 192.0.2.0/24, tagged 65001:100 (FDE90064). Its high half is neither 0
# nor the route server's 65000, so it is an ordinary community that names nobody and the
# route reaches both clients.
action=send:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF003D02000000224001010040020602010000FDE94003040101010140050400000064C00804FDE9006418C00002

# conn=2, the EXCLUDED client -- AS 65002, on 127.0.0.2. Every assertion about the exclusion
# and its limit lives here.
# THE POINT OF THIS TEST: the withdrawal half REACHES the excluded client.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:contains=18C63364
# CONTROL 1: the fence reaches this session too, so the assertion above cannot pass by way
# of some unrelated message.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=2:contains=18C00002
# THE ASSERTION: 10.0.0.0/24 never reaches this client, in any framing.
reject=bgp:conn=2:pattern=180A0000

# conn=3, the INCLUDED client -- AS 65003, on 127.0.0.3. CONTROL 2.
# The control community names 65002 only, so this client receives BOTH halves. ze splits
# the mixed shape before sending it (RFC 7606 Section 5.1), withdrawal first, so the two
# halves arrive as two messages in that order, and the fence follows.
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=1:contains=18C63364
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=2:contains=180A0000
expect=bgp:conn=3:seq=3:contains=18C00002
EOF_PEER

# Observer: hold the daemon up until the fence has reached the excluded client, which proves
# the mixed UPDATE ahead of it was already decided, then count what each client was sent.
tmpfs=control-community-withdraw.run:mode=755:terminator=EOF_RUN
#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Sequence shutdown behind the fence route reaching the excluded client."""
import sys
from ze_api import API, runtime_fail

EXCLUDED = '127.0.0.2'
INCLUDED = '127.0.0.3'


def real_updates(api, peer):
    """UPDATEs written to peer, End-of-RIB markers excluded.

    updates-sent counts every UPDATE written, the initial-sync EoR included
    (IncrUpdatesSent, reactor/reactor_notify.go sees only the message type).
    eor-sent is the separate marker counter, so the difference is route traffic.
    """
    return api.peer_counter('updates-sent', peer=peer) - api.peer_counter('eor-sent', peer=peer)


def main():
    api = API()
    api.declare_done()
    api.wait_for_config()
    api.capability_done()
    api.wait_for_registry()
    api.ready()

    # quiesce FIRST so the polls behind it are a safety net rather than the
    # mechanism: it drains the forward pool in one RPC.
    api.quiesce()

    # The included client gets three: the withdrawal, the announcement, the fence.
    if not api.wait_until(lambda: real_updates(api, INCLUDED) >= 3, attempts=40):
        runtime_fail('included client was not sent both halves and the fence')
    print('OK: included client received both halves and the fence', file=sys.stderr)

    # The excluded client gets two: the withdrawal and the fence. Two, not three: three
    # would mean the announcement leaked, which the reject rule also catches on the wire.
    if not api.wait_until(lambda: real_updates(api, EXCLUDED) >= 2, attempts=40):
        runtime_fail('excluded client was not sent the withdrawal and the fence')
    if real_updates(api, EXCLUDED) != 2:
        runtime_fail('excluded client was sent more than the withdrawal and the fence')
    print('OK: excluded client received the withdrawal and the fence, nothing else', file=sys.stderr)

    api.dispatch('request shutdown')
    api.wait_for_shutdown()


if __name__ == '__main__':
    main()
EOF_RUN

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal adj-rib-in {
		use bgp-adj-rib-in
	}
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	external control-community-withdraw {
		run ./control-community-withdraw.run
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			receive [ update-received state ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}

	peer excluded-client {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			# LOAD-BEARING: the RFC 7947 policy branch in forwardUpdateCore is gated on
			# facts.rsClient, which is this leaf. Without it the control community
			# decides nothing and every assertion below passes against a broken build.
			rs-client true
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			receive [ update-received state ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}

	peer included-client {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.3
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.3
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65003
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			rs-client true
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
		attach process rs {
			receive [ update-received state open-received refresh ]
			send [ update ]
		}
		attach process adj-rib-in {
			receive [ update-received state ]
			send [ update ]
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

# test-relax: three background ze-peer processes become ONE holding three connections, so
# the cmd= count drops from four to two. No assertion is removed: every expect= and the
# reject= moved to conn=2 / conn=3 of that one peer, and the file gains the await_eor barrier
# they need to be sound. The barrier is per accepted BATCH (peer_connmap.go), so it cannot
# span processes -- one process is the only shape in which these assertions are not a race.
cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=30s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic recovered
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
